WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.480
Data engineering, step by step.

2
00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:04.258
Let's watch data move.

3
00:00:05.208 --> 00:00:07.104
A school library needs three

4
00:00:07.104 --> 00:00:09.468
tables: members, loans and books.

5
00:00:09.848 --> 00:00:12.326
A member can borrow many books, so

6
00:00:12.326 --> 00:00:14.576
each member has many loans, and

7
00:00:14.576 --> 00:00:16.256
each loan names one book.

8
00:00:17.206 --> 00:00:19.492
The database reads the table first.

9
00:00:19.872 --> 00:00:22.506
WHERE keeps the marks of fifty or more.

10
00:00:22.886 --> 00:00:25.238
SELECT picks the name and the mark.

11
00:00:25.618 --> 00:00:27.742
ORDER BY puts the highest first,

12
00:00:27.922 --> 00:00:29.938
and LIMIT keeps the top three.

13
00:00:30.888 --> 00:00:33.228
Which classes average sixty or more?

14
00:00:33.608 --> 00:00:36.176
JOIN puts each mark beside its student.

15
00:00:36.556 --> 00:00:38.908
GROUP BY gathers the rows by class.

16
00:00:39.288 --> 00:00:41.184
HAVING keeps the groups that

17
00:00:41.184 --> 00:00:42.636
average sixty or more.

18
00:00:43.016 --> 00:00:45.692
Then SELECT gives each class its average.

19
00:00:46.642 --> 00:00:49.156
Which classes have a mark under sixty?

20
00:00:49.536 --> 00:00:51.096
WHERE keeps those marks.

21
00:00:51.476 --> 00:00:53.780
SELECT takes only the class, and

22
00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:55.610
DISTINCT removes the repeats.

23
00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:58.792
Rain gauges send readings all day,

24
00:00:58.972 --> 00:01:01.180
one at a time: that's a stream.

25
00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:03.792
The check catches a reading below

26
00:01:03.792 --> 00:01:06.108
zero and sets it aside, and the

27
00:01:06.108 --> 00:01:08.394
counters show how many got through.

28
00:01:09.344 --> 00:01:11.690
Attendance moves in batches: every

29
00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:14.492
evening, the whole day's register goes at

30
00:01:14.492 --> 00:01:14.828
once.

31
00:01:15.208 --> 00:01:18.196
One record has a missing name, so it goes

32
00:01:18.196 --> 00:01:21.010
back to be fixed, then joins the others.

33
00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:24.774
Type the formula once, in the first row.

34
00:01:25.154 --> 00:01:27.524
Then fill it down, and the sheet

35
00:01:27.524 --> 00:01:29.084
works out every average.

36
00:01:30.034 --> 00:01:31.648
Before we count anything,

37
00:01:31.828 --> 00:01:33.064
we clean the data.

38
00:01:33.444 --> 00:01:35.652
Here is a typo, a blank price,

39
00:01:35.832 --> 00:01:37.350
and a row typed twice.

40
00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:39.584
We fix the typo and remove

41
00:01:39.584 --> 00:01:40.700
the repeated row.

42
00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:44.074
Tables and links, a query step by

43
00:01:44.074 --> 00:01:46.552
step, a pipeline and a clean sheet:

44
00:01:46.732 --> 00:01:48.172
that's data engineering.
